    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate with **Corbin Carroll** standing as the unequivocal anchor of the offense. With an elite score of 87.14, he benefits from a favorable matchup against Littell, and while the opposing battery presents a minor speed-depletion risk, his positional efficiency in the two-hole remains the highest-ceiling play on the board. He is bolstered by high-upside secondary options like **Gabriel Moreno** and **Brice Turang**, who both eclipse the 70-point threshold. **Brice Turang** in particular is a priority start, as the park factors and a plus matchup against Agnos create a perfect storm for production. Meanwhile, **James Wood** remains a must-start despite facing a left-handed arm; his elite 0.401 xwOBA keeps his efficiency comfortably high at 75.08, demonstrating that his raw talent currently supersedes the usual platoon-aversion concerns.\n\nThe optimization engine faced its toughest dilemma regarding **Travis Bazzana**. Sitting at a 50.93 score, he was the final cut for the active roster, narrowly squeezed out by the sheer volume of high-score utility options facing weaker arms like Kochanowicz and Imai. While **Travis Bazzana** offers valuable lead-off volume, his raw projection lagged behind the platoon-advantaged **Vinnie Pasquantino**, who faces a stiffer test in Ryan but maintains a stronger efficiency profile. Risks for the day are relatively contained, though investors should note that **Seiya Suzuki** is facing a challenging 1.88-second pop-time catcher, which complicates his upside. Similarly, while **Josh Naylor** and **Cole Young** are in excellent spots, we remain vigilant regarding the wind-aided conditions; any shift in the wind direction could dampen the power-hitting potential for this lineup. With **Mickey Moniak** sidelined on the IL, we continue to rely on the current depth to maintain our category leads, specifically in the stolen base department.",
